/-!
# Interpolation of operators of restricted strong type

This file was automatically generated by Claude.

It proves an interpolation theorem of Marcinkiewicz–Stein–Weiss type for operators of
restricted strong type. It depends on Mathlib only.

## Setting

* `(X, μ)`, `(Y, ν)` are arbitrary measure spaces (no σ-finiteness is needed); inputs are functions
  `f : X → E` with values in a normed additive group `E` (e.g. `E = ℂ`), and
  `T : (X → E) → Y → ℝ≥0∞` takes nonnegative extended real values.
* `D : Set (X → E)` is a class of a.e. strongly measurable functions which is
  `IsLevelTruncationClosed`: `f 𝟙_{s < ‖f‖ ≤ t} ∈ D` for `f ∈ D`, `0 ≤ s < t`.
* `IsDisjointSublinearOn T D ν`: if `f ∈ D`, `f_k ∈ D` (`k ∈ ℤ`) have pairwise disjoint supports
  and `f = ∑_k f_k` pointwise, then `T f ≤ ∑_k T f_k` `ν`-a.e.
* `HasRestrictedStrongTypeOn T D q M μ ν`: for every measurable `S` with `μ S < ∞`, every `c ≥ 0`
  and every `g ∈ D` with `‖g‖ ≤ c 𝟙_S`, `‖T g‖_{L^q(ν)} ≤ M c μ(S)^(1/q)`.

## Main result

* `eLpNorm_le_of_restrictedType`: if `T` is of restricted strong type `(p₀, p₀)` and `(p₁, p₁)`
  on `D` with constants `M₀, M₁ > 0`, where `0 < p₀ < p < p₁ < ∞` (in particular for
  `1 ≤ p₀`), then for every `f ∈ D` with `T f` a.e. strongly measurable,
  `‖T f‖_{L^p(ν)} ≤ C(p₀,p₁,p) M₀^(1-θ) M₁^θ ‖f‖_{L^p(μ)}`, `1/p = (1-θ)/p₀ + θ/p₁`,
  with the explicit constant `C(p₀,p₁,p) = rtiConst p₀ p₁ p` depending only on `p₀, p₁, p`
  (geometric mean of the constants).
* `rti_lintegral_tsum_rpow_le`: the underlying abstract summation estimate for a family
  `(g_k)_{k ∈ ℤ}` of nonnegative functions with distributional bounds at two exponents.

## Proof

Decompose `f = ∑_k f_k`, `f_k = f 𝟙_{E_k}`, `E_k = {2^k < ‖f‖ ≤ 2^(k+1)}`, so that
`T f ≤ G := ∑_k T f_k` a.e. and, by the restricted strong-type bounds and Chebyshev,
`ν{T f_k > t} ≤ t^(-pᵢ) (2Mᵢ)^(pᵢ) 2^(k pᵢ) μ(E_k)` for `i = 0, 1`. A discrete layer-cake
inequality gives `∫ G^p ≤ 2^p ∑_j τ_j^p ν{G > τ_j}`, `τ_j = λ 2^j`. At height `τ_j` the
threshold is split among the levels with geometric weights, `t_{jk} = W⁻¹ 2^(-γ|k-j|) τ_j`
(`∑_k t_{jk} = τ_j`), so that `ν{G > τ_j} ≤ ∑_k ν{T f_k > t_{jk}}`; the `p₁`-bound is used for
`k ≤ j` and the `p₀`-bound for `k > j`. Each term is at most
`K 2^(-δ|k-j|) 2^(k p) μ(E_k)`, and summing first over `j` (a discrete Schur-type estimate)
leaves `2^p Ψ K ∑_k 2^(k p) μ(E_k) ≤ 2^p Ψ K ∫ ‖f‖^p`. Finally the scaling `λ` is chosen to
balance the two contributions, which yields the geometric mean `M₀^(1-θ) M₁^θ`.
In fact only the weak-type consequence of the restricted strong-type hypotheses is used.

## References

* E. M. Stein, G. Weiss, *Introduction to Fourier Analysis on Euclidean Spaces*, Princeton
  University Press, 1971, Chapter V, §3 (restricted weak type and the Marcinkiewicz theorem).
* L. Grafakos, *Classical Fourier Analysis*, Graduate Texts in Mathematics 249, Springer,
  §1.4 (interpolation for operators of restricted weak type).
-/

